WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.520
The Joker has been portrayed&nbsp;
in many movies over the years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.040
As time marches on, more cinematic&nbsp;
takes on the Joker are created,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.680
and new rules for the character's silver screen&nbsp;
portrayals are established. But despite these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.560
different interpretations, these are the rules&nbsp;
Batman's nemesis has to follow in every film.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.200
The Joker is known for many things:&nbsp;
Dating his psychiatrist, killing Robin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.400
and even attempting to copyright fish. Yet there's&nbsp;
still one thing that makes the Joker really stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.520
out on the grim streets of Gotham: His look. The&nbsp;
criminal mastermind is known by fans for wearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.400
purple with green hair - a color combo that&nbsp;
appears at least once in each film portrayal.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.320
Some Batman villains are also recognized&nbsp;
for their wild style, like the Riddler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.040
the Mad Hatter, and Polka Dot Man. But as the&nbsp;
years have gone on, Gotham's bad guys have come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.960
to favor monochromatic tactical armor and subdued&nbsp;
gangster suits, in keeping with the noir aesthetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.920
established in the early comics. By contrast, the&nbsp;
Joker bursts off the page and onto the screen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.040
colors rarely worn by villains, especially in&nbsp;
movies. When the Joker is on screen with Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.240
the audience's eyes are inevitably drawn to the&nbsp;
circus-y tableau that is the Joker's hair, suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.400
and garish makeup. To do the legendary&nbsp;
villain justice, a film portrayal of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.440
Joker requires a get-up so loud, it takes away&nbsp;
attention from the guy the movie's named after.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.280
The Joker's sense of humor not&nbsp;
only gives the character his name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.440
but also defines his crimes. The Joker&nbsp;
may have failed as a stand-up comedian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.400
but he still builds entire evil plots around&nbsp;
punchlines, puns, and one-liners. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.480
he might be looking to kill you, but the Joker&nbsp;
is also definitely out to make you laugh.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.960
Each new Joker actor is tasked with finding their&nbsp;
unique take on the Joker's particular sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.480
humor. Heath Ledger's portrayal in 2008's The Dark&nbsp;
Knight is a sterling example of an actor blending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.360
abhorrent behavior and comedic timing to draw some&nbsp;
genuine, if regretful, laughs from the audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.040
Whether it's the dry delivery of a quip&nbsp;
after he brains a criminal with a pencil…

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:55.250
"I'm gonna make this pencil disappear."

00:01:55.875 --> 00:01:57.193
"Ta-da!"

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.160
...or the dark physical comedy Ledger brings&nbsp;
to the Joker's frustration with a detonator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.920
the beloved actor set the bar. It's&nbsp;
twisted comedic beats like these that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.000
give the character his staying power. When he's&nbsp;
on screen, no one knows whether to be ready for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.320
something hilarious, something horrific,&nbsp;
or the Joker's own blended brand of both.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.120
Any worthwhile villain needs a theme song.&nbsp;
Vader has "The Imperial March," the shark in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.520
Jaws has John Williams' "Theme From Jaws,"&nbsp;
and characters in any Martin Scorsese movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.360
have The Rolling Stones. These songs alert&nbsp;
the audience that the villain has arrived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.800
With the special exception of Jack Nicholson's&nbsp;
art-defacing dance party in 1989's Batman, set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.760
to the tune of Prince's "Partyman," the Joker's&nbsp;
theme isn't a song - it's his devilish cackle.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.720
The laugh, which hits an octave that immediately&nbsp;
registers as maniacal, is so iconic that The New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.360
York Times published an entire feature on the&nbsp;
pressure actors feel to get it right on screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.720
Most of the performers who've played the&nbsp;
part over the years credit one man with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.760
mastering the Joker laugh: Mark Hamill, who&nbsp;
voiced Joker in Batman: The Animated Series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.000
As Hamill revealed to the Times, his&nbsp;
audition script bore an explicit command:

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.360
"Don't think Nicholson."

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.400
So he drew inspiration from Amadeus, the&nbsp;
Blue Meanies from 1968's Yellow Submarine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.440
and Dwight Frye's 1930s Dracula movies.&nbsp;
Hamill's performance may only be vocal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.960
but he set the benchmark for what&nbsp;
audiences think a Joker laugh should be.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.560
The '80s was a formative decade for the Joker and&nbsp;
Batman. Frank Miller's The Dark Knight Returns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.760
Batman: The Killing Joke by Alan Moore and Brian&nbsp;
Bolland gave the Caped Crusader a new and gritty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.880
edge that would come to define his corner of the&nbsp;
DC universe. Miller's work focuses on the dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.520
long-term relationship formed between the&nbsp;
two men, having them fight to the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.320
in a Tunnel of Love ride. Moore and Bolland's&nbsp;
tale explores the insanity these characters share:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:01.040
Where Batman stares unblinkingly into chaos,&nbsp;
the Joker has flung himself straight in.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.440
These depictions didn't just give audiences&nbsp;
an interesting new conflict - they also turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.720
Joker into a nigh-perfect Batman villain, who is&nbsp;
the hero's opposite in nearly every sense. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.440
dynamic has vividly colored how movies portray&nbsp;
the relationship between these foes. No matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.400
who plays him, the Joker will do anything to sow&nbsp;
chaos, and Batman will do almost everything to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.920
stop him. Even in 2019's Joker, Arthur Fleck has&nbsp;
a conflicted connection with young Bruce Wayne.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.160
While the villain acting as the hero's dark&nbsp;
mirror is a well-worn trope, it's rarely done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.640
so perfectly. Audiences are always entertained&nbsp;
when, as Ledger's Joker says in The Dark Knight,

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:40.080
"...an unstoppable force&nbsp;
meets an immovable object."

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.800
While the Joker might not be able to bring&nbsp;
himself to kill the man behind the cowl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.520
he'll certainly knock off the people closest to&nbsp;
the Caped Crusader. Even when he's not targeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.040
Batman, it's the Wayne family he's after, such as&nbsp;
the grudge he holds against them in 2019's Joker.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:59.280
Ever since comic fans voted for the&nbsp;
Joker to kill Jason Todd in 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.800
movie adaptations have heavily leaned into the&nbsp;
personal toll he exacts upon his foe. In 1989's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.640
Batman, the Joker is the man who murders Wayne's&nbsp;
parents. In 1993's Batman: Mask of the Phantasm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:16.000
the Joker almost kills the love of Batman's life.&nbsp;
In 2000's Batman Beyond: Return of the Joker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.960
it's revealed that he kidnapped Tim Drake, comic’s&nbsp;
third Robin, and brainwashed him as a child to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:26.640
grow up to be the next Joker. Most famously, in&nbsp;
2008's The Dark Knight, the Joker kidnaps Batman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:32.160
childhood friend and old flame, blows her up, and&nbsp;
in the process, makes Harvey Dent into Two-Face.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.720
At this point in the filmography, Joker is&nbsp;
the grand marshal of Batman's tragedy parade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.440
The movie franchise has explored many tones,&nbsp;
from camp to thriller noir to pure action.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.200
The characters need something to ground them&nbsp;
through these many fluctuations - something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.280
audiences can immediately grasp. The Joker&nbsp;
threatening Batman with the loss of loved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.520
ones is intimately recognizable to anyone,&nbsp;
no matter who's in the director's chair.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.880
Many great actors have portrayed&nbsp;
Bruce Wayne over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.080
but no matter how talented the&nbsp;
performer in the batsuit might be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.480
whenever Batman and the Joker share the&nbsp;
screen, the actor playing the Joker upstages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:09.600
his pointy-eared counterpart. Take 1989's&nbsp;
Batman: Jack Nicholson's manic charisma fuels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.000
an iconic performance that draws attention&nbsp;
from Michael Keaton's reserved Bruce Wayne.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.360
When news broke that Heath Ledger would&nbsp;
be cast in the role for The Dark Knight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.080
angry fans decried the decision, but later&nbsp;
changed their tune. Upon the film's release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:25.680
legendary movie critic Roger&nbsp;
Ebert described Ledger's Joker as,

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.520
"The key performance in the movie."

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.960
He followed that sentence by predicting&nbsp;
Ledger's posthumous Oscar win.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.280
The potential for prestigious actors to run&nbsp;
away with the role has become so entrenched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.280
in the zeitgeist that Todd Phillips built&nbsp;
his entire standalone Joker film around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.520
Joaquin Phoenix's bizarre, difficult,&nbsp;
and ultimately Oscar-winning performance.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.440
Unlike some beloved characters,&nbsp;
the Joker's aesthetic and attitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.360
adjust to the current moment. By contrast,&nbsp;
Batman may receive some suit upgrades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.400
but he retains roughly the&nbsp;
same tabulation of grimaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.880
batarangs, and tactical leather. The&nbsp;
Joker evolves while Batman stands still.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.840
Jack Nicholson's Joker dances to Prince,&nbsp;
and his gang's coordinated berets, Ray-Bans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.960
and parachute pants give their enterprise a&nbsp;
distinctly late-'80s sense of chic. In Batman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.400
Mask of the Phantasm, Hamill's Joker wears a&nbsp;
sleek purple suit and a dangerously toothy grin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.920
in keeping with the retro noir&nbsp;
aesthetic of the '90s cartoon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:22.880
Something interesting happened in 2008:&nbsp;
Heath Ledger's Joker is notably grimy looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:28.000
and also adopts a criminal attitude that&nbsp;
reflects the era's post-9/11 fear of terrorism.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.240
Recently, the Joker's portrayals have shifted&nbsp;
for a generation that grew up with the internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.440
Jared Leto's Joker in Suicide Squad is a Patreon&nbsp;
page away from being a criminal influencer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.960
while Joaquin Phoenix's Joker taps into societal&nbsp;
fear of the sort of lonely, disenfranchised men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:46.560
who have become closely associated with&nbsp;
the darkest corners of the internet.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.640
Part of the Joker's appeal is his&nbsp;
status as the ultimate wild card;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.440
he is likely to betray anyone and anything at a&nbsp;
moment's notice. From comic appearances to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:00.160
small screen to the silver screen, portrayals of&nbsp;
the Joker excel when he's acting unpredictably.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:04.880
His status as a villain allows writers the freedom&nbsp;
to have him make choices the hero never would,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:09.360
while his reputation as a maniac keeps everyone,&nbsp;
even his alleged allies, on their toes.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.040
When he's not throwing his own henchmen&nbsp;
into harm's way for self-preservation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:17.680
he's setting Two-Face loose on the same men who&nbsp;
paid the Joker to kill Batman. Or, if he's keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.720
it simple, he's burning mountains of cash. In an&nbsp;
interview with the BBC, director Christopher Nolan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:28.000
explained how the Joker's sporadic behavior was&nbsp;
baked into every aspect of Ledger's performance:

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.720
"A lot of it was about unpredictability,&nbsp;
and I think he wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:33.600
play his cards a little close to the chest."

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.360
That sort of mystique makes a&nbsp;
villain instantly fascinating and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:40.400
endlessly watchable - even if done&nbsp;
through eye-shielding fingers.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.120
Any cinematic portrayal of the Joker&nbsp;
worth its salt features a grand entrance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.520
These arrivals aren't just cool - they also&nbsp;
announce the Joker's presence in a grandiose way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.000
that immediately raises the narrative stakes.&nbsp;
For example, Ledger's entrance in The Dark Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.400
is a tightly constructed double-cross. By&nbsp;
the time the final bank robber realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:01.760
that the Joker has instructed all the&nbsp;
men on the job to execute each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:06.720
the audience has a clear idea of the type of Joker&nbsp;
they're dealing with. Ledger's Joker is a madman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:12.000
yes, but the opening robbery also demonstrates&nbsp;
his ruthless intelligence and calculated plotting.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.560
In Joker, Joaquin Phoenix's complete take on the&nbsp;
character doesn't arrive until the third act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.120
When he does finally appear in full regalia,&nbsp;
his entrance isn't punctuated with a fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.440
scene or a heinous crime. Instead, it's&nbsp;
a dance number. Phoenix's Joker debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.920
set to Gary Glitter's "Rock and Roll Part 2,"&nbsp;
couldn't be more different from Ledger's reveal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.840
however, both entrances set new&nbsp;
standards for grand Joker arrivals.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:38.240
Humor alone does not, a good Joker&nbsp;
make - he should also be terrifying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:41.680
While the Joker is an inherently&nbsp;
vivacious and charismatic villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:44.960
his staying power comes from the way&nbsp;
he blends that charisma with horror.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:49.920
Consider the hostage tape/snuff film the Joker&nbsp;
sends Gotham news stations in the first act of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.800
Dark Knight. With its homemade style, purposefully&nbsp;
amateurish editing, and its unmixed audio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.120
the terrifying tape appears to have rolled in&nbsp;
from another movie. It's deeply disturbing.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:03.120
Later, when Ledger's Joker holds a dinner&nbsp;
party hostage on the hunt for Harvey Dent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.120
everyone on screen and in the theater feels&nbsp;
their palms sweat as they become aware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.200
the Joker's intentions for his victim. It's&nbsp;
moments like these that are essential to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:15.680
character's power over moviegoers. Plenty&nbsp;
of villains have flair, many crack jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.840
and most have an army of dispensable goons&nbsp;
who rarely receive first and last names.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:24.000
But the Joker is one of those special villains&nbsp;
whose bark is worse than his formidable bite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.400
The Joker's ability to evoke a visceral response&nbsp;
from the audience whenever he's on screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:32.240
is what has ingrained the character in the&nbsp;
public consciousness. Fans of every age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.400
remember childhood hairs standing up on the backs&nbsp;
of their necks when they hear his sinister laugh.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:41.520
There's one fundamental rule movie portrayals of&nbsp;
the Joker must follow that trumps all the others:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.960
The Joker can never kill Batman.&nbsp;
One of the most interesting things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:48.320
about the Joker is his desire&nbsp;
to keep his arch-nemesis alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:51.520
He even admits as much in The Dark&nbsp;
Knight's interrogation scene, saying,

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:55.600
"I don't want to kill you,&nbsp;
what would I do without you?"

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.240
For most villains, this approach would really&nbsp;
deflate the danger they present to the hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.880
In contrast, the Joker's desire to continue&nbsp;
the fight makes him seem even more dangerous.

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.600
The never-ending struggle between Batman and the&nbsp;
Joker has become part of the franchise's appeal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:14.000
The Joker often remarks that life without Batman&nbsp;
would be boring, and the audience can't help but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:18.880
feel the same way. He is constantly finding ways&nbsp;
to almost break the Bat, and thus, constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:23.920
finding new ways to keep audiences interested.&nbsp;
This homicidal will-they-won't-they dynamic makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.160
their relationship truly one-of-a-kind,&nbsp;
and fans everywhere are grateful for it.

